module

public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Smooth.Field
public import StacksFrontier.RingTheory.Smooth.Field
public import StacksFrontier.RingTheory.KrullDimension.FiniteTypeOverField
public import StacksFrontier.RingTheory.RingHom.FinitePresentation

/-!
# Field extensions are filtered colimits of complete intersections

Let `K/k` be a field extension. Then `K` is a filtered colimit of global complete intersection
algebras over `k`, and if `K/k` is separable, `K` is a filtered colimit of smooth `k`-algebras.

Both statements reduce to the following: every finite subset `E ⊆ K` is contained in a
`k`-subalgebra `A ⊆ K` which is a global complete intersection (resp. smooth) over `k`. Such
subalgebras are of finite type, so any two of them lie in a third one, and the family of all of
them is directed under inclusion with union `K`.

In the separable case let `L ⊆ K` be the subfield generated by `E`. It is a finitely generated
subextension of a separable extension, hence separably generated, hence formally smooth and
therefore separable over `k`, so by Lemma 037X it is the fraction field of a smooth `k`-subalgebra
`R ⊆ L`. Inverting a common denominator `g ∈ R` of the elements of `E` gives the smooth
`k`-subalgebra `R_g ⊆ L ⊆ K`, which contains `E`.

In general the subalgebra `A` is built by adjoining the elements of `E` one at a time, keeping
track of a presentation `k[X_1, …, X_n]/(f_1, …, f_c) ≅ A` together with a transcendence basis of
`A` over `k` with `n - c` elements. To adjoin `e ∈ K` to such a `B ⊆ K`, let `F ⊆ K` be the
subfield generated by `B`.
* If `e` is transcendental over `F`, then `B[e] ≅ B[Y]`, which adds one variable, no relation and
  one element to the transcendence basis.
* If `e` is algebraic over `F`, write the coefficients of its minimal polynomial over `F` with a
  common denominator `g ∈ B`; the minimal polynomial is then the image of a monic `μ ∈ B_g[Y]`,
  and division by `μ` shows that the kernel of `B_g[Y] → K`, `Y ↦ e`, is `(μ)`. So
  `B_g[e] ≅ B_g[Y]/(μ)`, which adds two variables and the two relations `gT - 1` and `μ`; the
  transcendence basis of `B` stays one of `B_g[e]`, which is algebraic over `B`.
At the end `A` is a domain of finite type over `k` whose fraction field has transcendence degree
`n - c`, so `dim A = n - c` by Lemma 00P0, and `A` is a global complete intersection over `k`.

## Main results

* `Subalgebra.directedOn_isGlobalCompleteIntersectionOver_and_exists_mem`: the
  `k`-subalgebras of `K` which are global complete intersections over `k` form a directed family
  with union `K`.
* `Subalgebra.directedOn_smooth_and_exists_mem`: if `K/k` is separable, the smooth
  `k`-subalgebras of `K` form a directed family with union `K`.
* `Subalgebra.exists_finset_subset_isGlobalCompleteIntersectionOver`,
  `Subalgebra.exists_finset_subset_smooth`: every finite subset of `K` lies in such a subalgebra.
* `Polynomial.ker_eval₂RingHom_eq_span_of_monic`: a monic polynomial `μ` vanishing at `e`, such
  that no nonzero polynomial of smaller degree vanishes at `e`, generates the kernel of
  evaluation at `e`.

## Implementation notes

A filtered colimit of `k`-subalgebras of `K` along inclusions is their directed union, so the
statement that `K` is a filtered colimit of global complete intersection (resp. smooth)
`k`-algebras is rendered as: the global complete intersection (resp. smooth) `k`-subalgebras of
`K` form a directed family whose union is `K`. This is the form the source's proof establishes.

A global complete intersection over `k` is `IsGlobalCompleteIntersectionOver`, whose dimension
condition is `dim A + c = n` for a presentation with `n` variables and `c` relations. Separability
of a possibly transcendental extension is `Algebra.IsSeparableExtension` (Definition 030O).

The source proves the general case with a single transcendence basis `x_1, …, x_d` of the
subfield generated by `E`, followed by a tower of monic minimal polynomials `P_1, …, P_r` and one
denominator `h`. Here the elements of `E` are adjoined one at a time as described above; each
transcendental element plays the role of one `x_i`, each algebraic one that of one `P_i`
together with part of `h`, and the regular sequence `P_1, …, P_r` is replaced by the count of
variables against relations, which is what the dimension condition needs.

